 ## Overview
-Volume Raycasting (Ray Driven) is a direct volume rendering method that samples along rays through the entire dataset to render three-dimensional scalar fields [S1, unofficial]. It differs from splatting by accumulating samples interpolated between voxels surrounding the ray [S2, unofficial]. While it builds on the standard front-to-back compositing of [Standard Ray Casting](12643-standard-ray-casting.md), this specific implementation variant is often characterized by its simplicity, sometimes omitting interpolation in favor of direct voxel access, and supporting specific modes like limited depth raycasting [S3].
